# MedPlatform - stable naming and permissions for the ADS1299 front-end.
#
# /dev/iio:deviceN is numbered in probe order. On a board with one IIO device
# today it is always 0, and on the day a second one appears - a temperature
# sensor, an accelerometer, anything - the acquisition service silently opens
# the wrong converter. BRINGUP_STM32MP2.md §11 records the general form of this
# lesson: a device name is always a race, never a fact. So the platform
# addresses the front-end by a name only this front-end can claim, and
# eeg.conf's device.address names the symlink.
#
# The match is on the IIO device's own "name" attribute, which ti-ads1299 sets
# from the ID register the chip answered with. That makes the link an assertion
# about silicon that replied, not about a driver that loaded.
SUBSYSTEM=="iio", KERNEL=="iio:device*", ATTR{name}=="ads1299-*", \
    SYMLINK+="med-afe-eeg0", OWNER="root", GROUP="root", MODE="0660"

# 0660 root:root, and not a dedicated group.
#
# The plan asked for "by group, not 0666", and the reason to answer it with
# root ownership instead is a layering one: the only consumer is
# eeg-acquisition.service, which runs as root inside a systemd sandbox that
# already denies every device it is not explicitly granted. Creating a system
# group here would put an OS-policy fact (who exists on this system) inside a
# front-end layer, which is the boundary this layer exists to respect. If a
# non-root acquisition service ever arrives, the group is declared by
# meta-med-distro and this rule gains a GROUP= - one line, in the right place.

# The bridge, for the USB link. No node to name - the interrupt counter lives
# in sysfs - so this rule exists only to make the bridge's attributes
# discoverable by a stable path for the jitter and sample-loss measurements.
SUBSYSTEM=="hid", DRIVER=="mcp2210", TAG+="med-afe-bridge"
